					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kid-f" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2782678); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2782678&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606380249" alt="Kid F" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2782678)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kid-f" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2782678); return false;" >Kid F</a></span> <span class="odate time_1495532747 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 May 2017 09:45</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2829899">
									

<p>Unfortunately, I didn't enjoy this. I find the base concept quite interesting, but the execution is lacking, especially in the interview logs. The language used by the Foundation researchers during the interviews feels a little unprofessional, such as the final line of the last interview. It doesn't mesh well with how I'd imagine they act.</p>
<p>The narrative route of the anomaly seeking to destroy all other languages feels a little pedestrian, a pretty standard, straightforward development, and the reveal of its desire for vengeance against the loss of its 'brethren' fell flat for me. It feels like an afterthought of a conclusion - it's playing the narrative too safely, in my eyes.</p>
<p>The second interview seems fairly pointless to me. The danger the anomaly poses is already made apparent in both the description and the first interview, so the Euclid-Keter discussion just seems like entirely extraneous detail, especially considering that it isn't brought up again. It can be summed up like this: &quot;The anomaly is really dangerous!&quot; &quot;Eh, kind of.&quot;</p>
<p>I honestly thought this was building up to a containment breach, or some kind of scenario where the Foundation pays for its negligence. Instead, it just fizzles out.</p>
<p>On the other hand, like I said, I like the base idea, as well as the tie-in to SCP-3022 and alternate realities. The containment procedures, especially the first paragraph, serve as a well-crafted hook, so kudos for that.</p>

							</div>

								
					
