					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/novaedearx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1310897); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1310897&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606380249" alt="NovaeDeArx" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1310897)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/novaedearx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1310897); return false;" >NovaeDeArx</a></span> <span class="odate time_1495810642 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 May 2017 14:57</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2833419">
									

<p>I also felt like the fact that this is a self-aware, self-modifying, intentionally manipulative language wasn't exploited enough.</p>
<p>Let's say that it doesn't just modify itself, but that it also can modify other languages co-located in the same brain. Now we've got to question every piece of information provided by a speaker, including all of its effects and history.</p>
<p>So now you're set up for a 3rd act twist where this is discovered, and that we don't know how much of what we (the readers) know is false. This could be played with quite a bit to great effect, and it could even be that the whole language thing is a bluff, and that it's actually a verbally transmitted memetic effect that has already been transmitted to the other researchers.</p>

							</div>

								
					
